Problem Statement. Existing ZSAD/FSAD methods Zhu et al. ([2025](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30140#bib.bib36 "Fine-grained abnormality prompt learning for zero-shot anomaly detection")); Zhou et al. ([2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30140#bib.bib29 "AnomalyCLIP: object-agnostic prompt learning for zero-shot anomaly detection")); Zhu and Pang ([2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30140#bib.bib67 "Toward generalist anomaly detection via in-context residual learning with few-shot sample prompts")) typically require auxiliary datasets to adapt VLMs to AD. Specifically, given auxiliary data 𝒟aux={Xtrain,Ytrain}\mathcal{D}\_{\text{aux}}=\{X\_{\text{train}},Y\_{\text{train}}\}, where Xtrain={xi}i=1NX\_{\text{train}}=\{x\_{i}\}\_{i=1}^{N} denotes the training images, Ytrain={yi}i=1NY\_{\text{train}}=\{y\_{i}\}\_{i=1}^{N} denotes the corresponding binary labels, and each sample xix\_{i} satisfies yi∈{0,1}y\_{i}\in\{0,1\} with 0 and 11 represent normal an anomalous samples respectively, a pretrained VLM-based anomaly scoring function fVLM​(xi,Prompt)f\_{\text{VLM}}(x\_{i},\text{Prompt}) is fine-tuned using 𝒟aux\mathcal{D}\_{\text{aux}} and then directly applied to yield an anomaly score for samples from unseen test data 𝒟test\mathcal{D}\_{\text{test}}. In contrast, our work operates in a fully training-free manner, requiring neither auxiliary data training nor parameter adaptation. In particular, our goal is to develop a generalizable MLLM-driven anomaly reasoning agent f MLLM​(xi,Prompt,Tool,Memory)f\_{\text{ MLLM}}(x\_{i},\text{Prompt},\text{Tool},\text{Memory}) that can effectively generalize across different unseen test datasets 𝒯={𝒟test1,𝒟test2,⋯,𝒟testm}\mathcal{T}=\{\mathcal{D}^{1}\_{\text{test}},\mathcal{D}^{2}\_{\text{test}},\cdots,\mathcal{D}^{m}\_{\text{test}}\} under both zero-shot and few-shot settings, where each test dataset 𝒟testi={Xtesti,Ytesti}\mathcal{D}^{i}\_{\text{test}}=\{X^{i}\_{\text{test}},Y^{i}\_{\text{test}}\} is drawn from a distinct application domain and f MLLMf\_{\text{ MLLM}} does not require any training. In the zero-shot setting, no target-domain reference samples are available during inference. In contrast, FSAD additionally provides a small set of normal reference samples from the target domain, denoted as 𝒫={p1,p2,…,pk}\mathcal{P}=\{p\_{1},p\_{2},...,p\_{k}\}, where kk is typically a small number (e.g., k≪Nk\ll N). The resulting model is expected to directly produce a hard-label prediction, ie, “anomalous” or “normal”, providing a global prediction of whether the query image contains any abnormal content.

